    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 260013\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n513 PM MST Fri Sep 25 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 00Z Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Quiet conditions under mostly clear skies can be expected through \n  Saturday, with near to slightly above normal temperatures. \n\n- Starting Sunday, moisture will surge back into the region with cloudy\n  skies and eventual chances for light rain later in the day. \n\n- Monday and Tuesday may bring good chances for rain and possibly some\n  strong thunderstorms. Heavy rainfall will also be possible, \n  particularly across the Arizona high terrain. \n\n- Temperatures will cool to below normal during the first half of\n  next week with highs from the mid eighties to the mid nineties.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nLatest Water vapor satellite imagery shows the mid-level low \nfeature that brought unsettled conditions during the last 24-36 \nhours quickly lifting to the northeast, with westerly flow aloft \nenveloping much of the region. This westerly flow will advect much\ndrier air into the region through Saturday with PWATs bottoming \nout under one inch. Therefore, expect fairly quiet weather with \nmostly clear skies and zero rain/storm chances. Temperatures \nduring the next couple of days will be near to slightly above \nnormal. Afternoon highs today will top out in the mid to upper 90s\nacross the south-central AZ lower deserts and upper 90s to lower \n100s across the western deserts, with slightly warmer readings for\nSaturday.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nA significant weather event could be shaping up for next Monday\ninto Tuesday as models are beginning to come to a better \nconsensus for a seasonably strong upper level trough moving \nthrough our region. The biggest uncertainty still involves how \nmuch moisture will be available when the trough does move through,\nbut it is looking more optimistic compared to last night. \n\nThis next event is expected to start with strong upper level\nmoisture advection from the southwest which will actually come\nfrom the remnants of the decaying tropical system Odalys. Odalys\nis forecast to drift northward over the next day or two before\ngetting absorbed into the westerlies as a trough digs\nsoutheastward off the coast of California. We can anticipate\nplenty of higher level moisture and clouds overspreading our area\nbeginning Saturday evening and continuing through much of Sunday.\nThis moisture fetch is likely to be very consistent for 18-24\nhours with gradual top-down moistening of the atmosphere. By\nSunday mid to late morning, some areas may start to see some\nsprinkles, or even very light rain, but no measurable rainfall. It\nwould not be surprising to see a fairly narrow band of light rain\ndeveloping by Sunday evening somewhere across the area, but\namounts are expected to remain very light. \n\nAlso starting Sunday, Hurricane Polo is forecast to approach\nsouthern Baja before eventually tracking to the northeast across\nBaja and the Gulf of California most likely later on Monday. The\nlatest NHC track of Polo keeps it well to our south as it is\nexpected to fall apart quickly as it moves onshore into southern\nSonora Mexico. We are not worried about any direct impacts from\nPolo, but it is becoming increasingly more likely it will help\ndirect additional moisture northward into our region, specifically\nin the low to mid levels. This moisture will be necessary for any\ndecent rainfall to occur early next week, so any big deviations in\nthe forecast moisture will affect our rain chances for Monday and\nTuesday.\n\nAlthough the details are expected to change, forecast confidence\nfor a decent weather/rain event continues to increase. As the bulk\nof the upper level moisture from the remnants of Odalys mostly \npushes into New Mexico later on Sunday, we should begin to see \nmoisture stream out of the south southwest from Polo by Monday \nmorning. The best moisture is expected to bypass our region to the\nsoutheast, but we still should see good moisture working into \nmost of Arizona early on Monday, right ahead of the approaching \nupper level trough. Ensemble mean PWATs of 200% of normal are \nshown for the eastern 2/3rds of Arizona on Monday with the bulk of\nthis moisture falling in the low and mid levels, just where it \nwould be needed for a decent rain event. \n\nThe upper level trough will play a very important role early next\nweek as it looks likely to provide strong forcing on Monday and \nTuesday. Upper level ascent is expected to be quite good, along \nwith very good 30-45 kts of southwesterly upslope forcing within \nthe mid levels. It is still too early to have a great idea on the \ndetails of the evolution of the rainfall, but it could start in \nearnest by Monday morning. NBM PoPs increase areawide by Monday \nmorning with 30-50% chances across the western deserts to 50-75% \nfrom Phoenix and north and east of Phoenix. These PoPs basically \nhold through Monday night before gradually tapering off from west \nto east on Tuesday. We may see fairly steady upslope showers and \nisolated thunderstorms across south-central and eastern Arizona \nMonday through early Tuesday.\n\nWe are currently looking at three possible scenarios for early\nnext week. First, and the most likely outcome (~60% chance), is \nfor a modestly strong upper level trough to push through Monday \ninto Tuesday with very adequate, but not tropical level moisture \ngetting pulled into our area right ahead of the passing trough. \nThis scenario would support widespread rainfall amounts of \n0.10-0.30\" over the lower deserts to 0.5-1.0\" over the high \nterrain north and east of Phoenix. This should also involve a \nperiod of strong thunderstorms due to quite good wind shear. \nSecond, (~20% chance) would keep the best moisture to our south \nand/or a much weaker and faster trough. In this scenario, we would\nexpect much more scattered rainfall with lower desert amounts \nunder a 0.25\" and higher terrain amounts more like 0.25-0.75\". At \nthe other end of the spectrum (~20% chance) would leave us with \nthe upper end of potential moisture combining with a stronger and \nslower trough. This would bring a longer rain event with lower \ndesert rain amounts averaging from 0.2-0.5\" over the western \ndeserts to 0.75-1.50\" for the south-central Arizona lower deserts.\nAs much as 2-3\" would also be very possible over the high terrain\nnorth and east of Phoenix. This stronger event is also likely to \ninvolve a period of strong to severe storms. \n\nIt will be another day or two until we have a good idea on the \nexpected impacts for this upcoming event, but as stated above\nthere is at least some possibility of a high impact event. We \nwill continue to monitor the model trends and provide the latest \nupdates with this upcoming weather event. \n\nTemperatures are expected to take a big dip early next week with\nlower desert highs falling to as low as the mid 80s in the \nPhoenix area to the low to mid 90s across the western deserts. \nOnce the weather system ejects to the east by next Wednesday, we \nshould fall back into a drier and warmer pattern. \n \n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0015Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo aviation concerns are expected during the TAF period. Winds\nwill follow familiar light and diurnal trends with some moments of\nvariability during directional shifts. A period of southerly\ncrosswinds (KPHX and KDVT) is also anticipated tomorrow late \nmorning/early afternoon prior to the westerly shift. Skies will be\nmostly clear in the vicinity of the terminals through this \nevening ahead of high clouds moving in early tonight.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo aviation concerns are expected during the next 24 hours. Winds\nwill favor light and diurnal trends, with periods of VRB\nconditions tonight into Saturday morning. Clear skies will give\nway to increasing high clouds this evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nMuch drier air settling into the region will result in quiet\nweather and near zero rain chances through Saturday along with \nnear to slightly above normal temperatures. Rain chances will \nincrease once again starting Sunday as moisture levels increase.\nThere is the potential for a more widespread wetting rainfall \nevent early next week. MinRHs today will bottom out between 25-45%\nacross the eastern districts and 15-25% across the western \ndistricts before dropping further to 15-25% in most areas on \nSaturday. As moisture levels increase starting Sunday, MinRHs \nwill rebound above 20% across most areas. Winds will generally \nfollow the typical diurnal tendencies with limited to no afternoon\ngusts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Berislavich/RW \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Kuhlman\n",
